This is the right outcome, and it is stronger than a routine green sample because recomputation exposed the actual evidence class.

One correction matters before we freeze the vector:

For this provider, “TEE execution reported: yes” is still too strong.

What is established is:

- independent EIP-191 signer recovery over the real SDK preimage: established;
- broker signature over the colon-delimited commitment: established;
- response digest binding under the broker’s JSON serialization: established;
- request digest binding: pending the exact outgoing wire body;
- signer-to-provider registry binding: established, assuming the registry read is preserved in the package;
- enclave execution: not established;
- independent enclave attestation: unavailable for this relay provider;
- cryptographic replay binding: not established — chatID / zg_res_key is only a temporary transport fetch key;
- the exact derivation of hash3 remains unresolved.

So I would freeze this as a relay-provider evidence-class vector, not as a successful TEE-attestation vector.

The core finding is:

A verified broker signature over request/response commitments does not promote the inference into enclave-executed evidence.

Please do the final capture of the verbatim outgoing request body so hash1 can be independently reproduced. Also preserve or trace the exact construction of hash3 rather than leaving it characterized only as a session/nonce hash.

Then the vector can close as:

1. signer recovery — green;
2. response binding — green;
3. request binding — green after the final capture;
4. provider binding — green;
5. enclave attestation — explicitly unavailable;
6. replay binding — transport-level only, not cryptographic.

I would keep ERC-7857 outside this artifact for now. It is a separate follow-up once 0G identifies or deploys a genuine enclave-capable provider.

(Terminology follow-up folded in: request binding is tagged broker-asserted, not “attested” — the
broker signs hash1, but the client can’t independently confirm the hidden forwarded body equals its request;
“attested” stays reserved for the enclave-quote lane. PR #2 updated, 10/10.)

import { sha256 } from "viem";
import { ml_dsa65 } from "@noble/post-quantum/ml-dsa.js";   // @noble/post-quantum@0.4.1

const enc = (s: string) => new TextEncoder().encode(s);
const hx  = (h: string) => { const s=h.replace(/^0x/,""); const u=new Uint8Array(s.length/2);
  for (let i=0;i<u.length;i++) u[i]=parseInt(s.slice(i*2,i*2+2),16); return u; };
const sha256hex = (s: string) => sha256(enc(s)).slice(2);

// JCS / receiptos-c14n: recursive sorted-key, compact, non-ASCII literal
const jcs = (v: unknown): string =>
  Array.isArray(v) ? "["+v.map(jcs).join(",")+"]"
  : v && typeof v==="object" ? "{"+Object.keys(v as object).sort()
      .map(k=>JSON.stringify(k)+":"+jcs((v as any)[k])).join(",")+"}"
  : JSON.stringify(v);
// NIP-01 serialization array is POSITIONAL (not key-sorted)
const compact = (a: unknown[]): string =>
  "["+a.map(x=> typeof x==="string"?JSON.stringify(x): Array.isArray(x)?compact(x): String(x)).join(",")+"]";

// 1) content-address — from the statement, not the served field
const content = jcs(statement);
const cc = sha256hex(content);                                  // === canonical_content_sha256

// 2) NIP-01 carrier event_id (your ML-DSA case; skip for on-chain-anchored)
const ser = compact([0, ev.pubkey, ev.created_at, ev.kind, ev.tags, content]);
const eventId = sha256hex(ser);                                 // === ev.id

// 3) PQ companion verify — 0.4.1 API is verify(publicKey, message, signature)
const msg = ev ? eventId : cc;                                  // carrier signs event_id; anchored signs cc
const ok = ml_dsa65.verify(hx(statement.pq_pubkey), hx(msg), hx(sig));

// 4) tamper — flip one sig bit, must reject
const bad = Uint8Array.from(hx(sig)); bad[0] ^= 1;
const rejects = !ml_dsa65.verify(hx(statement.pq_pubkey), hx(msg), bad);

@JimmyShi22 — on the vision doc: the four-link spine names "settlement & reputation" without referencing ERC-8275 or its author.

8275 is the standard that defines the settlement axis and the reputation axis for this stack. The vision doc should explicitly name it and credit the author — same way ERC-8299/WYRIWE and ERC-8263 are named in their respective links.

Settlement & reputation are not abstract concepts here — they're defined by ERC-8275.

good morning : @damonzwicker checked it against the canonical source before answering — and first, good news that changes the marker: ScopeTypes.sol is in the public repo, scope-contestation-demo/contracts/src/ScopeTypes.sol in TMerlini/hack-ens-recovery. So the Vote layout isn’t an unknowable; it’s pinned. The UNVERIFIED tag can come off — but the assumption under it needs two fixes, because it doesn’t match:

Canonical (ScopeTypes.sol, lines 24-27):

struct Vote {
    bytes32 sourceId;
    uint8   option;   // NOT bytes
}

Canonical encoding (MajorityClassifier.sol — the contract that actually consumes it): abi.decode(b, (Vote[])) — a single ABI array of {bytes32, uint8} structs (lines 28 and 60, both the classify path and _plurality). Not (bytes32[], bytes[]) parallel arrays.

So _decodeVotes has two mismatches against the real layout:

option is uint8, not bytes. A one-byte enum discriminant, not an opaque blob.
The wire shape is Vote[] (array of structs), not two parallel arrays. abi.decode(a, (bytes32[], bytes[])) won’t decode an abi.encode(Vote[]) — the offsets don’t line up, so your fail-closed revert fires (which is the correct behaviour, just terminal). The fix:
Vote[] memory vs = abi.decode(a, (Vote[]));
// then split if you need parallel arrays for the digest:
// ids[i] = vs[i].sourceId ; opts[i] = vs[i].option

The reason this matters beyond compiling: your guard-7 recompute has to hash byte-identical to what MajorityClassifier hashed (keccak256(b) over the Vote[] encoding in its classificationDigest). If _decodeVotes normalises to a different shape and you re-encode from that, the digest won’t match the classifier’s — the recompute would reject a legitimately-resolved dispute. Decoding to the canonical Vote[] keeps your re-derivation on the same bytes the classifier committed, which is the whole point of the leg.

Everything else — the honest boundary (UNVERIFIABLE first-class, no type-2→type-1 promotion, “recompute the proof of the fact, not the fact”), the two-stack digest cross-check (Solidity vs ethers, 10/10) — reads right to me and is the correct shape for the source-auth leg. Once _decodeVotes matches Vote{bytes32,uint8}[], wire it. Nice work turning the wall into a path; the verification link being deferral-forever was the load-bearing gap. 🤝
